WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:07.998
FAYE MOSS: Hello and welcome to Superstars in History , the talk show where we chat with famous people from

2
00:00:07.999 --> 00:00:11.095
the past. I'm your host,

3
00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:18.034
Faye Moss. Today's guest was a scientist who pioneered the scientific method with his work on

4
00:00:18.035 --> 00:00:27.926
physics. This superstar also spent a lot of time gazing at the stars, which changed our entire understanding of the solar system.

5
00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:35.111
Unfortunately, discovering the secrets of the skies got him in a lot of trouble down on Earth! Please

6
00:00:35.112 --> 00:00:54.899
help me welcome Galileo Galilei. GALILEO

7
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.797
GALILEI: Hello,

8
00:00:56.798 --> 00:00:59.798
Faye. FAYE MOSS: I see you

9
00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:03.319
brought a telescope! I can't wait to hear all about what you saw

10
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.828
with it. But first, tell us a little bit about your

11
00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:12.746
early years. GALILEO GALILEI: I was born in 1564 in the town of

12
00:01:12.747 --> 00:01:15.933
Pisa in Tuscany. My father was a musician

13
00:01:15.934 --> 00:01:22.956
and music theorist. As a child I played the lute and considered a career in music like my father, but I also thought about becoming

14
00:01:22.957 --> 00:01:27.705
a painter. When I was 11 years old, I was sent to be educated in a

15
00:01:27.706 --> 00:01:31.769
monastery. However, my father had bigger plans for me than becoming a

16
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:37.046
monk. FAYE MOSS: Oh? Did he want you to become a

17
00:01:37.047 --> 00:01:42.328
revolutionary scientist? GALILEO GALILEI: Not exactly. He enrolled me at the University of Pisa

18
00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:49.688
to study medicine. My father's career in music had not provided much income for our family, so he wanted me to have a well-paying career as

19
00:01:49.689 --> 00:01:58.171
a doctor. I found the studies boring. In those days learning medicine was just a matter of memorizing and repeating what authority figures

20
00:01:58.172 --> 00:02:06.169
said. But once I began listening to a variety of lectures on campus, I discovered mathematics, which opened a new world to me!

21
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:13.508
I begged my father to let me switch from medicine to math, and though he opposed at first, he eventually agreed. FAYE

22
00:02:13.509 --> 00:02:16.311
MOSS: But you didn't become a

23
00:02:16.312 --> 00:02:20.825
mathematician. You're famous for your scientific observations of

24
00:02:20.826 --> 00:02:25.409
nature. GALILEO GALILEI: Well, I often said, the book of nature is written

25
00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:29.484
in mathematics. Math is a way to understand

26
00:02:29.485 --> 00:02:33.561
the world. It helped me explain my observations and make

27
00:02:33.562 --> 00:02:41.652
predictions. FAYE MOSS: Today we often think of you as the father of the scientific method, especially with

28
00:02:41.653 --> 00:02:45.615
your motion studies. Can you tell us about your

29
00:02:45.616 --> 00:02:51.857
scientific process? GALILEO GALILEI: I came up with ideas by carefully observing nature, then I designed experiments to

30
00:02:51.858 --> 00:02:57.712
test my ideas. For example, Aristotle thought heavier objects fall faster

31
00:02:57.713 --> 00:03:00.890
than lighter objects. I had long suspected that

32
00:03:00.891 --> 00:03:09.291
he was wrong. In order to see if objects fall at the same speed, no matter their mass, I dropped things from high places and noted when they

33
00:03:09.292 --> 00:03:14.994
landed. I also rolled lead balls down inclined planes to study

34
00:03:14.995 --> 00:03:20.100
acceleration. I recorded my observations and used math to explain them.

35
00:03:20.101 --> 00:03:25.828
FAYE MOSS: Speaking of observations, let's talk about

36
00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:29.248
that telescope. Did you invent the

37
00:03:29.249 --> 00:03:35.065
first telescope? GALILEO GALILEI: No. Someone in the Netherlands had made one first—they called

38
00:03:35.066 --> 00:03:39.752
it a spyglass. It was originally used for spotting ships or seeing far

39
00:03:39.753 --> 00:03:43.352
distances on Earth. Though I had no plans to work from, I built

40
00:03:43.353 --> 00:03:46.210
my own. And I must say that mine was

41
00:03:46.211 --> 00:03:51.700
much better! The first telescopes could magnify things three times larger than what the naked eye could

42
00:03:51.701 --> 00:03:57.051
see. But I was able to improve the magnification at first to eight, and eventually beyond that.

43
00:03:57.052 --> 00:04:02.832
Even though I didn't invent the telescope, I was the first to use one to study the heavens. FAYE

44
00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:06.831
MOSS: Tell us what you saw up there and what it

45
00:04:06.832 --> 00:04:10.695
meant. GALILEO GALILEI: It was astonishing to be able to see the planets

46
00:04:10.696 --> 00:04:16.530
up close. I saw mountains on the moon! Most people had thought the moon had a

47
00:04:16.531 --> 00:04:23.220
smooth surface. Oh, and I saw that Jupiter had moons too. I studied the sun and discovered

48
00:04:23.221 --> 00:04:28.450
sunspots. Most interestingly, I discovered that Venus has phases, just like the moon.

49
00:04:28.451 --> 00:04:34.256
By observing these phases, I was able to disprove the popular idea that Venus orbited Earth.

50
00:04:34.257 --> 00:04:39.277
These observations convinced me that the astronomer Nicolaus Copernicus was right. FAYE

51
00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:44.948
MOSS: Copernicus? He argued that Earth revolved around the sun,

52
00:04:44.949 --> 00:04:48.773
right? GALILEO GALILEI: Yes. This topic was hotly debated in

53
00:04:48.774 --> 00:04:52.746
my day. Most people agreed with the second-century Greek

54
00:04:52.747 --> 00:04:58.411
astronomer Ptolemy. His system put Earth at the center of the universe, with the sun and other planets orbiting around

55
00:04:58.412 --> 00:05:01.414
it. Even I liked Ptolemy's theory since it was simple.

56
00:05:01.415 --> 00:05:06.975
But after I began observing and studying the planets, it became clear that Copernicus was right.

57
00:05:06.976 --> 00:05:16.591
FAYE MOSS: Your work supporting Copernicus attracted some . . . um, negative attention from the Catholic

58
00:05:16.592 --> 00:05:21.214
Church. GALILEO GALILEI: The Church, like Ptolemy, believed Earth was the center of

59
00:05:21.215 --> 00:05:28.381
the universe. Church officials strongly thought the Bible supported this idea, and therefore my work was seen as challenging

60
00:05:28.382 --> 00:05:35.801
the Bible! So, for reasons that had more to do with faith than science, the Church tried to silence me starting in

61
00:05:35.802 --> 00:05:44.179
1610. FAYE MOSS: Weren't you afraid? Church leaders had burned other people at the stake for supporting

62
00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:49.168
Copernicus' idea! GALILEO GALILEI: Indeed they had. But I had scientific evidence

63
00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:57.846
on my side. As I said at the time, I did not think that the same God who gave us our senses, reason, and intelligence wanted us to abandon

64
00:05:57.847 --> 00:06:04.285
their use. I believed that science would prevail, and the Church leaders would accept my findings when I explained

65
00:06:04.286 --> 00:06:06.907
them. Alas, I was wrong about

66
00:06:06.908 --> 00:06:15.341
that. FAYE MOSS: In 1633 they arrested you and tried you for heresy, the act of holding a belief different from the

67
00:06:15.342 --> 00:06:21.869
Church's doctrine. GALILEO GALILEI: I was put in prison, threatened with torture, and forced to sign a statement

68
00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:26.699
recanting my views. After I did that, I was released from prison but kept

69
00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:30.585
under house arrest. At least I was able to have visitors with whom I could talk

70
00:06:30.586 --> 00:06:38.238
about science. I was also allowed books and even managed to write one, though I had to do that in secret as I was forbidden to write new

71
00:06:38.239 --> 00:06:42.906
books! House arrest wasn't too bad. It certainly could have been worse.

72
00:06:42.907 --> 00:06:46.688
FAYE MOSS: Well, I don't want to keep you

73
00:06:46.689 --> 00:06:53.016
captive here! Thank you so much for joining us today. We'll let you get back to history

74
00:06:53.017 --> 00:06:58.126
now. GALILEO GALILEI: Thank you, Faye. I enjoyed

75
00:06:58.127 --> 00:07:02.609
talking with you. FAYE MOSS: Galileo lived out the rest of his

76
00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:13.974
life under house arrest. With the help of his friends, he managed to have his last book published in Holland. Galileo died in 1642 at the

77
00:07:13.975 --> 00:07:23.552
age of 77. Galileo's confirmation of Copernicus' theory eventually became accepted by his detractors, including the

78
00:07:23.553 --> 00:07:27.330
Catholic Church. He didn't just make an impact

79
00:07:27.331 --> 00:07:35.686
on astronomy. With his innovative experiments and application of math to science, he paved the way for future

80
00:07:35.687 --> 00:07:44.433
scientists. Albert Einstein honored Galileo by calling him the father of modern

81
00:07:44.434 --> 00:07:50.945
science. I hope you enjoyed today's show. Be sure to tune in again next time!

